Exploratory observations
The dataset included 714 passengers, with a mean age of 29.7 years (SD = 14.53). Overall, 290 passengers (40.6%) survived, while 424 (59.4%) died. The sample included more males (n = 453, 63.4%) than females (n = 261, 36.6%), and third-class passengers represented the largest group (n = 355, 49.7%). Survival rates differed considerably across groups. Among females, 197 (75.5%) survived, compared with 93 (20.5%) males. Similarly, survival was highest among first-class passengers (n = 122, 65.6%) and lowest among third-class passengers (n = 85, 23.9%). These patterns suggest that sex and passenger class may be associated with survival.

